<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Crowfoot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1830–<a href="/years/1890">1890</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Isapo-Muxika</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Crowfoot was a chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siksika">Siksika</a>, one of the Blackfoot peoples of the northern plains. A noted warrior, he became a leading advocate of peace with the Canadian government as the bison disappeared, and he signed Treaty 7 in 1877, which ceded much of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberta">Alberta</a>. He kept the Blackfoot out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-rebellion">North-West Rebellion</a> of 1885.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/crowfoot.jpg" alt="Crowfoot" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alex Ross · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chief_Crowfoot.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Crowfoot was born to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kainai-nation">Kainai</a> parents, Istowun-ehʼpata (Packs a Knife) and Axkahp-say-pi (Attacked Towards Home). He was first called Shot-Close, later Bear Ghost and then Packs a Knife after his father was killed in a raid on the Crow when the boy was five. A year later his mother married Akay-nehka-simi (Many Names) of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siksika">Siksika</a>, among whom Crowfoot grew up. He fought in as many as nineteen battles and was often wounded, and his people called him Manistokos, father of the people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Crowfoot worked to keep good relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> and the North-West Mounted Police. Late in 1866 he stopped warriors from looting a train of company carts and killing its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> drivers and escorted the Métis back to Fort Edmonton. He refused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitting-bull">Sitting Bull</a>'s call to fight the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the police together, though he later made peace with the Sioux when they fled into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>.</p><p>At the Treaty 7 talks the government took him, wrongly, for the leader of the whole Blackfoot Confederacy. Red Crow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kainai-nation">Kainai</a> said he would sign if Crowfoot did, and on 21 October Crowfoot told his people: The police have protected us as the feathers of the bird protect it from the frosts of winter.</p><p>When rations were cut in 1881 and 1882 and the chief Bull Elk was arrested unjustly, Crowfoot confronted the police but sent his men home; Bull Elk was acquitted, and Edgar Dewdney replaced the agent Norman Macleod with Cecil Denny. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-rebellion">North-West Rebellion</a> of 1885 Crowfoot's adopted son, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cree">Cree</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poundmaker">Poundmaker</a>, was deeply involved, and Crowfoot sheltered Cree refugees in his camp while keeping his warriors out of the fighting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The farming programme that followed did not supply the tools and help it promised, and famine and disease killed most of Crowfoot's children. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poundmaker">Poundmaker</a> died while visiting him after his release from prison. Crowfoot died of tuberculosis at Blackfoot Crossing on 25 April 1890, and eight hundred of his people attended his funeral. His jacket, bow and pipe were returned from Exeter to the Blackfoot Crossing Historical Park in 2014, and Willie Dunn's film The Ballad of Crowfoot of 1968 was the first National Film Board of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> film by an Indigenous director.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Crowfoot” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Blackfoot had signed the Lame Bull Treaty with the United States in 1855; weakened by the Marias Massacre of 1870 and the near extinction of the bison, they signed Treaty 7 with Canada in 1877, negotiated by <b>Crowfoot</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/blackfoot-confederacy">Blackfoot Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rupert's Land. Died in Blackfoot Crossing, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.8,-112.91&amp;year=1869" aria-label="Blackfoot Crossing on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1980 401 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Crowfoot"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2430 671h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2430 671h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.8,-112.91&amp;year=1869">What was here in 1869? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
